  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 142 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nWhile warm advection will continue through the day Friday, moisture \nadvection will be in short supply until Friday evening. As the \nsurface low progresses further northeast into Canada, a plume of \nGulf moisture will make its way into the central US. This moisture \nwill be rather anomalous for mid-Novemember at nearly 1.0 inch, or \nwell over the 90th percentile. \n\nThis will set the stage for a quick and widespread rainfall event \nSaturday afternoon into overnight with the passage of a cold front. \nThere is increasing confidence in at least 0.5 inches of rainfall \nacross parts of central and eastern Iowa, with chances of 0.5in or \nmore at 50-80% along and west of a Mount Pleasant to Galena line. \nRainfall amounts east of this line, while lower, are still expected \nto near 0.25 inches. Synoptic lift along the front and marginal \ninstability will support chances for isolated rumbles of thunder \nSaturday night.\n\nBehind the cold front, more seasonable temperatures for late \nNovember are expected. Flow aloft will become zonal and fall into a \nprogressive pattern, which could set the stage for an active stretch \nof weather.",
